 ],
 "literal": "The blue water lily with arranged petals, nourished by a water channel, / does not wither even if the summer wind strikes it. / Like the weary bullocks rubbing against their yokes like slings, / like being arranged in a row, the herd of the salt-merchants' caravan, / due to lack of strength to split the withered branches, / the elephants fold their trunks and languish. / Even the forest will be pleasant if she comes with you.",
 "translation": "The blue water lily with rows of petals, nourished by a water channel, does not wither even when the hot summer wind strikes it. In the desert, elephants lack the strength to split the dry branches; they fold their trunks and languish, looking like the weary bullocks of a salt-merchant's caravan that rub against their sling-like yokes as they stand in a row. Yet, even such a harsh forest will be pleasant for her, if she comes along with you.",
 "modern": "நீர்க்காலால் நீர் ஊட்டப்பட்டு, வரிசையான இதழ்களைக் கொண்ட குவளை மலர், கோடைக்காலத்து வெப்பக் காற்று வீசினாலும் வாடாமல் இருக்கும். கவண் போன்ற நுகத்தடியில் தேய்ந்து வருந்தும் உமணர்களது வண்டிகளை இழுக்கும் எருதுகளின் வரிசையைப் போல, காய்ந்த மரக்கிளைகளை ஒடிப்பதற்கான வலிமை இல்லாததால், தும்பிக்கையை மடக்கிக் கொண்டு யானைகள் வருந்தும். இத்தகைய கொடிய காடும், அவள் உன்னோடு வந்தால் அவளுக்கு இனியதாகவே இருக்கும்.",
 "explanation": "The friend (tōḻi) speaks to the hero who is planning to depart on a journey through the harsh desert path to earn wealth, leaving the heroine behind. She describes the severe heat of the desert (pālai), where even mighty elephants fold their trunks in exhaustion, unable to break the dry branches, resembling a weary line of salt-merchants' bullocks. However, she reassures him that if he takes the heroine with him, the journey will not be painful for her. She uses an implied metaphor (uḷḷurai) of the blue water lily: just as the lily does not wither in the hot summer wind because it is constantly nourished by a water channel, the heroine will easily endure the desert heat if she is sustained by the constant flow of his love. Thus, the friend urges the hero to either take her along or refrain from leaving her.",
